{
  "dom_id": "step-q171825",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131900/assets/89a4eb747c82276f349350f48ac2b711e1a308702eb1c717fbeacf79dbdad84f.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠BAE={185}^{∘},$ $m∠BAD={120}^{∘},$ $m∠CAD={70}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$m∠CAE=$\n{{field-1}} ${}^{∘}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The measure of $∠DAE$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAE)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠BAD).$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nm∠DAE={185}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}={65}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add this to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131900/assets/fb782eecfe7280c5808c8f0a42dbe0daec4110d94aa373271a03311dd0a0ead0.png)\n\nWe can now find the measure of $∠CAE:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠CAE & =m∠CAD+m∠DAE \\\\  & ={70}^{∘}+{65}^{∘} \\\\  & ={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
